        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "2be498e8-81b7-59dd-83a7-4cbdcbe191a9",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Hibiscus waimeae, H. arnottianus, Hawaiian hibiscus, Urera kaalae\n- Places: Oʻahu, Molokaʻi, Hawaii\n- Habitats: coastal mesic forests, mixed mesic forests, wet forests\n- Associated species: Alyxia oliviformis, Antidesma platyphyllum, Asplenium kaulfusii, Athyrium, Canavalia\n\nFacts:\n- Hawaiian hibiscus are seven species of hibiscus regarded as native to Hawaii.\n- H. arnottianus of Oʻahu and Molokaʻi and H. waimeae are the only Hawaiian hibiscuses with white flowers.\n- H. waimeae inhabits coastal mesic forests, mixed mesic forests, and wet forests at elevations of 250 meters and above.\n- Urera kaalae is associated with maile (Alyxia oliviformis), hame (Antidesma platyphyllum), Asplenium kaulfusii, Athyrium species, and ʻāwikiwiki (Canavalia species).",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
